              {
                "type": "theorem",
                "id": "3",
                "title": "Theorem 3",
                "content": "If $a$ and $b$ are two positive integers with $a > b$ then G.C.D of $(a, b) = \\mathrm{GCD}$ of $(a - b, b)$.",
                "content_context": "If $a$ and $b$ are two positive integers with $a > b$ then G.C.D of $(a, b) = \\mathrm{GCD}$ of $(a - b, b)$.",
                "enrichment": {
                  "avatar_explanation": {
                    "teaching_style": "concept_builder",
                    "total_duration_estimate": "5 minutes",
                    "segments": [
                      {
                        "segment_id": "seg_001",
                        "type": "teaching",
                        "text": "Hey there! Have you ever played with building blocks? Well, in mathematics, we also have our own version of building blocks, and these are called numbers! But sometimes, we want to find the 'biggest' block that can perfectly fit into other blocks without leaving any gaps. That's what the G.C.D, or the Greatest Common Divisor, is all about. We're talking about finding that biggest block. Now, here's a neat little trick—when you have two numbers, like a and b, and a is bigger than b, the biggest block that fits into both of them (that's the G.C.D) is the same as the biggest block that fits into the numbers a - b and b. It's like saying you can chop off a piece from one block, and the biggest block won't change! Let's check it out.",
                        "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "fc_001",
                        "type": "flashcard",
                        "card_title": "Concept Example",
                        "front": "G.C.D of (16, 12) = G.C.D of (16 - 12, 12)",
                        "front_style": "real_world_example",
                        "avatar_line": "This is why it matters: By subtracting the smaller number from the larger, we simplify our task without changing the greatest divisor they share. So, the G.C.D of (4, 12) is the same as (16, 12), which is 4.",
                        "avatar_emotion": "thoughtful"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                }
              },
