                "type": "theorem",
                "id": "4",
                "title": "Theorem 4 (Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic) (without proof)",
                "content": "\"Every positive integer (except the number 1) can be represented in exactly one way apart from rearrangement as a product of one or more primes.\"\n\nThe fundamental theorem asserts that every composite number can be decomposed as a product of prime numbers and that the decomposition is unique. In the sense that there is one and only way to express the decomposition as product of primes.\n\nIn general, we conclude that given a composite number $N$, we decompose it uniquely in the form\n\n$N = p_{1}^{q_{1}} \\times p_{2}^{q_{1}} \\times p_{3}^{q_{1}} \\times \\dots \\times p_{n}^{q_{n}}$ where $p_{1}, p_{2}, p_{3}, \\ldots, p_{n}$ are primes and $q_{1}, q_{2}, q_{3}, \\ldots, q_{n}$ are natural numbers.\n\nFirst, we try to factorize $N$ into its factors. If all the factors are themselves primes then we can stop. Otherwise, we try to further split the factors which are not prime. Continue the process till we get only prime numbers.",
                "content_context": "\"Every positive integer (except the number 1) can be represented in exactly one way apart from rearrangement as a product of one or more primes.\"\n\nThe fundamental theorem asserts that every composite number can be decomposed as a product of prime numbers and that the decomposition is unique. In the sense that there is one and only way to express the decomposition as product of primes.\n\nIn general, we conclude that given a composite number $N$, we decompose it uniquely in the form\n\n$N = p_{1}^{q_{1}} \\times p_{2}^{q_{1}} \\times p_{3}^{q_{1}} \\times \\dots \\times p_{n}^{q_{n}}$ where $p_{1}, p_{2}, p_{3}, \\ldots, p_{n}$ are primes and $q_{1}, q_{2}, q_{3}, \\ldots, q_{n}$ are natural numbers.\n\nFirst, we try to factorize $N$ into its factors. If all the factors are themselves primes then we can stop. Otherwise, we try to further split the factors which are not prime. Continue the process till we get only prime numbers.",
                "enrichment": {
                  "avatar_explanation": {
                    "teaching_style": "concept_builder",
                    "total_duration_estimate": "5 minutes",
                    "segments": [
                      {
                        "segment_id": "seg_001",
                        "type": "teaching",
                        "text": "Hey there! Today, we're diving into a special rule about numbers called the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic. Why do we have this rule? Well, it's super important because it tells us that no matter how complex a number is, we can always break it down into prime numbers in one unique way. Think of it like Lego blocks. No matter how complex your Lego tower is, it's made of the same basic Lego pieces, and there’s only one unique set of these pieces for each tower.",
                        "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "fc_001",
                        "type": "flashcard",
                        "card_title": "Concept Example",
                        "front": "Take the number 30. Its prime factors are 2, 3, and 5. So, 30 can be written as 2 x 3 x 5.",
                        "front_style": "real_world_example",
                        "avatar_line": "This is why it matters... No matter which prime numbers you use in different orders, you can't make the number 30 in any other prime combination. It's unique!",
                        "avatar_emotion": "thoughtful"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                }
              },
              {
                "type": "illustration",
                "id": null,
                "title": null,
                "content": "For example, if we try to factorize 32760 we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{l}\n32760 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 5 \\times 7 \\times 13 \\\\\n= 2^{3} \\times 3^{2} \\times 5^{1} \\times 7^{1} \\times 13^{1}\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThus, in whatever way we try to factorize 32760, we should finally get three 2's, two 3's, one 5, one 7 and one 13.",
                "content_context": "For example, if we try to factorize 32760 we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{l}\n32760 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 5 \\times 7 \\times 13 \\\\\n= 2^{3} \\times 3^{2} \\times 5^{1} \\times 7^{1} \\times 13^{1}\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThus, in whatever way we try to factorize 32760, we should finally get three 2's, two 3's, one 5, one 7 and one 13.",
