                "content_context": "Can the number $6^n$, $n$ being a natural number end with the digit 5? Give reason for your answer.",
                "enrichment": {
                  "avatar_explanation": {
                    "teaching_style": "step_by_step",
                    "total_duration_estimate": "4 minutes",
                    "segments": [
                      {
                        "segment_id": "seg_001",
                        "type": "teaching",
                        "text": "Let's explore the problem and see if the number 6 raised to the power of n, where n is a natural number, can end with the digit 5.",
                        "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "fc_001",
                        "type": "flashcard",
                        "card_title": "Working step",
                        "front": "Express $6^n$ as $(2 \\times 3)^n$",
                        "front_style": "math_formula",
                        "avatar_line": "First, we rewrite 6 raised to the n as the product of 2 and 3, both raised to the power of n: $6^n = (2 \\times 3)^n$. This simplifies our work and is the basic expression of the number.",
                        "avatar_emotion": "encouraging"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "seg_002",
                        "type": "teaching",
                        "text": "Now, we expand $(2 \\times 3)^n$ using the properties of exponents.",
                        "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "fc_002",
                        "type": "flashcard",
                        "card_title": "Exponential property",
                        "front": "Expand $(2 \\times 3)^n$ to $2^n \\times 3^n$",
                        "front_style": "math_formula",
                        "avatar_line": "According to the properties of exponents, we can express $(2 \\times 3)^n$ as $2^n \\times 3^n$. This shows each factor raised to the power of n separately.",
                        "avatar_emotion": "encouraging"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "seg_003",
                        "type": "teaching",
                        "text": "Next, we understand the implications of having 2 as a factor of $6^n$.",
                        "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "fc_003",
                        "type": "flashcard",
                        "card_title": "Factor implication",
                        "front": "Notice $2^n$ in $2^n \\times 3^n$",
                        "front_style": "math_formula",
                        "avatar_line": "Since we have $2^n$ as part of the expression, it indicates that $6^n$ has at least one factor of 2. This means $6^n$ is always an even number.",
                        "avatar_emotion": "encouraging"
                      },
