                        "avatar_emotion": "encouraging"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "seg_003",
                        "type": "teaching",
                        "text": "To find an integer solution, x = (15k + 1) / 3 must be an integer. Let's simplify it to see if that's possible.",
                        "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "fc_004",
                        "type": "flashcard",
                        "card_title": "Step 4: Break down the expression",
                        "front": "x = 5k + (1/3)",
                        "front_style": "math_formula",
                        "avatar_line": "We split (15k + 1)/3 into 5k + 1/3 to see if x can still be an integer.",
                        "avatar_emotion": "encouraging"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "seg_004",
                        "type": "teaching",
                        "text": "Now, observe that 5k is an integer for any integer k. But adding 1/3 prevents x from being an integer because (5k + 1/3) is not a whole number.",
                        "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "seg_005",
                        "type": "teaching",
                        "text": "Since x = 5k + 1/3 cannot be made an integer, there are no integer solutions to the equation 3x ≡ 1 (mod 15). Isn't math fascinating? Great job following along!",
                        "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                }
