            "section_number": "2.8",
            "section_title": "Series",
            "content": "The sum of the terms of a sequence is called series. Let \\(a_1, a_2, a_3, \\ldots, a_n, \\ldots\\) be the sequence of real numbers. Then the real number \\(a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \\cdots\\) is defined as the series of real numbers.\n\nIf a series has finite number of terms then it is called a Finite series. If a series has infinite number of terms then it is called an Infinite series. Let us focus our attention only on studying finite series.\n\nSum to \\(n\\) terms of an A.P.\nA series whose terms are in Arithmetic progression is called **Arithmetic series**.\n\nLet \\(a, a + d, a + 2d, a + 3d, \\ldots\\) be the Arithmetic Progression.\n\nThe sum of first \\(n\\) terms of a Arithmetic Progression denoted by \\(S_{n}\\) is given by,\n\n$$\nS_{n} = a + (a + d) + (a + 2d) + \\cdots + (a + (n - 1)d) \\quad \\text{...(1)}\n$$\n\nRewriting the above in reverse order\n\n$$\nS_{n} = (a + (n - 1)d) + (a + (n - 2)d) + \\cdots + (a + d) + a \\quad \\text{...(2)}\n$$\n\nAdding (1) and (2) we get,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{l}\n2S_{n} = [a + a + (n - 1)d] + [a + d + a + (n - 2)d] + \\cdots + [a + (n - 2)d + (a + d)] + [a + (n - 1)d + a] \\\\\n= [2a + (n - 1)d] + [2a + (n - 1)d + \\cdots + [2a + (n - 1)d] \\quad (n \\text{ terms}) \\\\\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\n$$\n2S_{n} = n \\times [2a + (n - 1)d] \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad S_{n} = \\frac{n}{2} [2a + (n - 1)d]\n$$\n\n**Note**\n\nIf the first term \\(a\\), and the last term \\(l\\) (\\(n^{\\text{th}}\\) term) are given then\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{l}\nS_{n} = \\frac{n}{2} [2a + (n - 1)d] = \\frac{n}{2} [a + a + (n - 1)d] \\quad (\\because l = a + (n - 1)d) \\\\\nS_{n} = \\frac{n}{2} [a + l]. \\\\\n\\end{array}\n$$",
            "content_context": "The sum of the terms of a sequence is called series. Let \\(a_1, a_2, a_3, \\ldots, a_n, \\ldots\\) be the sequence of real numbers. Then the real number \\(a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \\cdots\\) is defined as the series of real numbers.\n\nIf a series has finite number of terms then it is called a Finite series. If a series has infinite number of terms then it is called an Infinite series. Let us focus our attention only on studying finite series.\n\nSum to \\(n\\) terms of an A.P.\nA series whose terms are in Arithmetic progression is called **Arithmetic series**.\n\nLet \\(a, a + d, a + 2d, a + 3d, \\ldots\\) be the Arithmetic Progression.\n\nThe sum of first \\(n\\) terms of a Arithmetic Progression denoted by \\(S_{n}\\) is given by,\n\n$$\nS_{n} = a + (a + d) + (a + 2d) + \\cdots + (a + (n - 1)d) \\quad \\text{...(1)}\n$$\n\nRewriting the above in reverse order\n\n$$\nS_{n} = (a + (n - 1)d) + (a + (n - 2)d) + \\cdots + (a + d) + a \\quad \\text{...(2)}\n$$\n\nAdding (1) and (2) we get,\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{l}\n2S_{n} = [a + a + (n - 1)d] + [a + d + a + (n - 2)d] + \\cdots + [a + (n - 2)d + (a + d)] + [a + (n - 1)d + a] \\\\\n= [2a + (n - 1)d] + [2a + (n - 1)d + \\cdots + [2a + (n - 1)d] \\quad (n \\text{ terms}) \\\\\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\n$$\n2S_{n} = n \\times [2a + (n - 1)d] \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad S_{n} = \\frac{n}{2} [2a + (n - 1)d]\n$$\n\n**Note**\n\nIf the first term \\(a\\), and the last term \\(l\\) (\\(n^{\\text{th}}\\) term) are given then\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{l}\nS_{n} = \\frac{n}{2} [2a + (n - 1)d] = \\frac{n}{2} [a + a + (n - 1)d] \\quad (\\because l = a + (n - 1)d) \\\\\nS_{n} = \\frac{n}{2} [a + l]. \\\\\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\nExample 2.31\nFind the sum of first 15 terms of the A.P. \\(8, 7\\frac{1}{4}, 6\\frac{1}{2}, 5\\frac{3}{4}, \\ldots\\)\n\nSolution:\nHere the first term \\(a = 8\\), common difference \\(d = 7\\frac{1}{4} - 8 = -\\frac{3}{4}\\).\nSum of first \\(n\\) terms of an A.P. \\(S_{n} = \\frac{n}{2} [2a + (n - 1)d]\\)\n\\(\n\\begin{array}{l}\nS_{15} = \\frac{15}{2} [2 \\times 8 + (15 - 1)(-\\frac{3}{4})] \\\\\nS_{15} = \\frac{15}{2} [16 - \\frac{21}{2}] = \\frac{165}{4} \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\)\n\nExample 2.32\nFind the sum of \\(0.40 + 0.43 + 0.46 + \\cdots + 1\\).\n\nSolution:\nHere the value of \\(n\\) is not given. But the last term is given. From this, we can find the value of \\(n\\).\nGiven, \\(a = 0.40\\) and \\(l = 1\\), we find \\(d = 0.43 - 0.40 = 0.03\\).\n\\(\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\text{Therefore, } n = \\left(\\frac{l - a}{d}\\right) + 1 \\\\\n= \\left(\\frac{1 - 0.40}{0.03}\\right) + 1 = 21\n\\end{array}\n\\)\nSum of first \\(n\\) terms of an A.P. \\(S_n = \\frac{n}{2} [a + l]\\)\n\\(\n, \\quad n = 21. \\quad\n\\text{Therefore, } S_{21} = \\frac{21}{2} [0.40 + 1] = 14.7\n\\)\nSo, the sum of 21 terms of the given series is 14.7.\n\nExample 2.33\nHow many terms of the series \\(1 + 5 + 9 + \\ldots\\) must be taken so that their sum is 190?\n\nSolution:\nHere we have to find the value of \\(n\\), such that \\(S_n = 190\\).\nFirst term \\(a = 1\\), common difference \\(d = 5 - 1 = 4\\).\nSum of first \\(n\\) terms of an A.P.\n\\(\n\\begin{array}{l}\nS_n = \\frac{n}{2} [2a + (n - 1)d] = 190 \\\\\n\\frac{n}{2} [2 \\times 1 + (n - 1) \\times 4] = 190 \\\\\nn [4n - 2] = 380 \\\\\n2n^2 - n - 190 = 0 \\\\\n(n - 10)(2n + 19) = 0 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\)\nBut, \\(n = 10\\) as \\(n = -\\frac{19}{2}\\) is impossible. Therefore, \\(n = 10\\).\n\nExample 2.34\nThe \\(13^{\\text{th}}\\) term of an A.P. is 3 and the sum of first 13 terms is 234. Find the common difference and the sum of first 21 terms.\n\nSolution:\nGiven, the \\(13^{\\text{th}}\\) term \\(= 3\\) so, \\(t_{13} = a + 12d = 3\\) ... (1)\nSum of first 13 terms = 234  \\(\\Rightarrow S_{13} = \\frac{13}{2} [2a + 12d] = 234\\)\n\\(\n2a + 12d = 36 \\quad \\dots (2)\n\\)\nSolving (1) and (2) we get,  \\(a = 33\\) ,  \\(d = \\frac{-5}{2}\\)\nTherefore, common difference is  \\(\\frac{-5}{2}\\) .\nSum of first 21 terms  \\(S_{21} = \\frac{21}{2}\\left[2\\times 33 + (21 - 1)\\times \\left(-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\right] = \\frac{21}{2} [66 - 50] = 168.\\)\n\nExample 2.35\nIn an A.P. the sum of first  \\(n\\)  terms is  \\(\\frac{5n^2}{2} + \\frac{3n}{2}\\) . Find the  \\(17^{\\text{th}}\\)  term.\n\nSolution:\nThe  \\(17^{\\text{th}}\\)  term can be obtained by subtracting the sum of first 16 terms from the sum of first 17 terms\n\\(\nS_{17} = \\frac{5 \\times (17)^2}{2} + \\frac{3 \\times 17}{2} = \\frac{1445}{2} + \\frac{51}{2} = 748\n\\)\n\\(\nS_{16} = \\frac{5 \\times (16)^2}{2} + \\frac{3 \\times 16}{2} = \\frac{1280}{2} + \\frac{48}{2} = 664\n\\)\nNow,  \\(t_{17} = S_{17} - S_{16} = 748 - 664 = 84\\)\n\nExample 2.36\nFind the sum of all natural numbers between 300 and 600 which are divisible by 7.\n\nSolution:\nThe natural numbers between 300 and 600 which are divisible by 7 are 301, 308, 315, ..., 595.\nThe sum of all natural numbers between 300 and 600 is  \\(301 + 308 + 315 + \\dots + 595\\) .\nThe terms of the above series are in A.P.\nFirst term  \\(a = 301\\) ; common difference  \\(d = 7\\) ; Last term  \\(l = 595\\) .\n\\(\nn = \\left(\\frac{l - a}{d}\\right) + 1 = \\left(\\frac{595 - 301}{7}\\right) + 1 = 43\n\\)\n\\(\\because S_{8} = \\frac{n}{2} [a + l]\\) , we have  \\(S_{43} = \\frac{43}{2} [301 + 595] = 19264.\\)\n\nExample 2.37\nA mosaic is designed in the shape of an equilateral triangle, 12 ft on each side. Each tile in the mosaic is in the shape of an equilateral triangle of 12 inch side. The tiles are alternate in colour as shown in the figure. Find the number of tiles of each colour and total number of tiles in the mosaic.\n\nSolution:\nSince the mosaic is in the shape of an equilateral triangle of 12 feet, and the tile is in the shape of an equilateral triangle of 12 inch (1 feet), there will be 12 rows in the mosaic.\nFrom the figure, it is clear that number of white tiles in each row are 1, 2, 3, 4, ..., 12 which clearly forms an Arithmetic Progression.\nSimilarly the number of blue tiles in each row are 0, 1, 2, 3, ..., 11 which is also an Arithmetic Progression.\n\\(\n\\text{Number of white tiles} = 1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + 12 = \\frac{12}{2} [1 + 12] = 78\n\\)\n\\(\n\\text{Number of blue tiles} = 0 + 1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + 11 = \\frac{12}{2} [0 + 11] = 66\n\\)\n\\(\n\\text{The total number of tiles in the mosaic} = 78 + 66 = 144\n\\)\n\nExample 2.38\nThe houses of a street are numbered from 1 to 49. Senthil's house is numbered such that the sum of numbers of the houses prior to Senthil's house is equal to the sum of numbers of the houses following Senthil's house. Find Senthil's house number?\n\nSolution:\nLet Senthil's house number be \\(x\\).\nIt is given that \\(1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + (x - 1) = (x + 1) + (x + 2) + \\cdots + 49\\)\n\\(\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + (x - 1) &amp;= \\left[1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + 49\\right] - \\left[1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + x\\right] \\\\\n\\frac{x - 1}{2} [1 + (x - 1)] &amp;= \\frac{49}{2} [1 + 49] - \\frac{x}{2} [1 + x] \\\\\n\\frac{x(x - 1)}{2} &amp;= \\frac{49 \\times 50}{2} - \\frac{x(x + 1)}{2} \\\\\nx^2 - x &amp;= 2450 - x^2 - x \\Rightarrow 2x^2 = 2450 \\\\\nx^2 &amp;= 1225 \\Rightarrow x = 35\n\\end{aligned}\n\\)\nTherefore, Senthil's house number is 35.\n\nExample 2.39\nThe sum of first \\(n\\), \\(2n\\) and \\(3n\\) terms of an A.P. are \\(S_1\\), \\(S_2\\) and \\(S_3\\) respectively. Prove that \\(S_3 = 3(S_2 - S_1)\\).\n\nSolution:\nIf \\(S_1, S_2\\) and \\(S_3\\) are sum of first \\(n\\), \\(2n\\) and \\(3n\\) terms of an A.P. respectively then\n\\(\nS_1 = \\frac{n}{2} [2a + (n - 1)d], \\quad S_2 = \\frac{2n}{2} [2a + (2n - 1)d], \\quad S_3 = \\frac{3n}{2} [2a + (3n - 1)d]\n\\)\nConsider,\n\\(\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_2 - S_1 &amp;= \\frac{2n}{2} [2a + (2n - 1)d] - \\frac{n}{2} [2a + (n - 1)d] \\\\\n&amp;= \\frac{n}{2} \\left[ [4a + 2(2n - 1)d] - [2a + (n - 1)d] \\right]\n\\end{aligned}\n\\)\n\\(\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_2 - S_1 &amp;= \\frac{n}{2} \\times [2a + (3n - 1)d] \\\\\n3(S_2 - S_1) &amp;= \\frac{3n}{2} [2a + (3n - 1)d] \\\\\n3(S_2 - S_1) &amp;= S_3\n\\end{aligned}\n\\)\n\nExample 2.39\nThe sum of first $n$, $2n$ and $3n$ terms of an A.P. are $S_1$, $S_2$ and $S_3$ respectively. Prove that $S_3 = 3(S_2 - S_1)$.\n\nSolution:\nIf $S_1, S_2$ and $S_3$ are sum of first $n$, $2n$ and $3n$ terms of an A.P. respectively then\n$$\nS_1 = \\frac{n}{2} [2a + (n - 1)d], \\quad S_2 = \\frac{2n}{2} [2a + (2n - 1)d], \\quad S_3 = \\frac{3n}{2} [2a + (3n - 1)d]\n$$\nConsider,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_2 - S_1 &amp;= \\frac{2n}{2} [2a + (2n - 1)d] - \\frac{n}{2} [2a + (n - 1)d] \n&amp;= \\frac{n}{2} \\left[ [4a + 2(2n - 1)d] - [2a + (n - 1)d] \\right]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_2 - S_1 &amp;= \\frac{n}{2} \\times [2a + (3n - 1)d] \n3(S_2 - S_1) &amp;= \\frac{3n}{2} [2a + (3n - 1)d] \n3(S_2 - S_1) &amp;= S_3\n\\end{aligned}$$",
            "type": "section",
            "section_enrichment": {
              "avatar_explanation": {
                "teaching_style": "visual_analogy",
                "total_duration_estimate": "7 minutes",
                "segments": [
                  {
                    "segment_id": "seg_001",
                    "type": "teaching",
                    "text": "Alright, today we're going to explore the fascinating world of series, focusing on Arithmetic Progressions or A.P.s. Imagine you're collecting seashells on a beach. Each day, you collect one more seashell than the day before. If you keep doing this for a certain number of days, how many seashells will you have collected in total? This process of adding up all those seashells can be thought of as a series.",
                    "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                  },
                  {
                    "segment_id": "fc_001",
                    "type": "flashcard",
                    "card_title": "Understanding Series",
                    "front": "Seashell collection analogy",
                    "front_style": "real_world_example",
                    "avatar_line": "Why do we add up the number of seashells collected each day?",
                    "avatar_emotion": "encouraging"
                  },
                  {
                    "segment_id": "seg_002",
                    "type": "teaching",
                    "text": "In our example, the series we form by adding up the seashells each day is called a finite series because there's a clear end after a certain number of days. If instead, you kept collecting seashells forever, it would be an infinite series. For now, we'll focus only on finite series.",
                    "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                  },
                  {
                    "segment_id": "seg_003",
                    "type": "teaching",
                    "text": "A series becomes an Arithmetic Series when the collected terms form an Arithmetic Progression. That means each term is evenly spaced—like picking up 2 more seashells each day than the previous day.",
                    "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                  },
                  {
                    "segment_id": "seg_004",
                    "type": "teaching",
                    "text": "Now, if we want to find the total number of seashells you've collected in an Arithmetic Progression over 'n' days, we use a handy formula: S_n = n/2 * [2a + (n - 1)d], where 'a' is the number of seashells you collected on the first day, 'd' is the increase in seashells each day, and 'n' is the total number of days. This formula lets us add up all the terms quickly without listing every single one.",
                    "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                  },
                  {
                    "segment_id": "seg_005",
                    "type": "teaching",
                    "text": "Keep in mind that if you know the first and the last day's collection, another formula can be used: S_n = n/2 * (a + l), where 'l' is the number of seashells collected on the last day. It's just another way to arrive at the same total.",
                    "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                  },
                  {
                    "segment_id": "fc_002",
                    "type": "flashcard",
                    "card_title": "Key Formula",
                    "front": "S_n = n/2 * [2a + (n - 1)d]",
                    "front_style": "uml_diagram",
                    "avatar_line": "Can you find the sum of seashells you collected over 10 days if you pick 1 more than the previous day?",
                    "avatar_emotion": "encouraging"
                  },
                  {
                    "segment_id": "seg_006",
                    "type": "teaching",
                    "text": "A common pitfall is forgetting that 'd' can be negative, which means you're actually collecting fewer seashells as the days go by. Remember to double-check what 'a' and 'd' mean in your specific problem!",
                    "emotion": "cautionary"
                  }
                ]
              }
