                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "fc_001",
                        "type": "flashcard",
                        "card_title": "Working step",
                        "front": "Sum formula: $1 + 2 + 3 + \\ldots + n = \\frac{n(n + 1)}{2}$",
                        "front_style": "math_formula",
                        "avatar_line": "First, we recall the sum of the first n natural numbers, which is given by this formula. This formula is essential because it gives us a way to find the sum without adding each number individually.",
                        "avatar_emotion": "encouraging"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "seg_002",
                        "type": "teaching",
                        "text": "Let's apply this formula to our problem. We know the sum is 666, so we set the formula equal to 666.",
                        "emotion": "enthusiastic"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "fc_002",
                        "type": "flashcard",
                        "card_title": "Setting up the equation",
                        "front": "$\\frac{n(n + 1)}{2} = 666$",
                        "front_style": "math_formula",
                        "avatar_line": "Here, we have equated the formula for the sum to 666. This sets up an equation that we can solve to find n.",
                        "avatar_emotion": "encouraging"
                      },
                      {
                        "segment_id": "seg_003",
                        "type": "teaching",
                        "text": "Now, we need to get rid of the fraction. We can do this by multiplying both sides by 2.",
