                "content": "The sum of first $n$, $2n$ and $3n$ terms of an A.P. are $S_1$, $S_2$ and $S_3$ respectively. Prove that $S_3 = 3(S_2 - S_1)$.",
                "metadata": {
                  "solution": "If $S_1, S_2$ and $S_3$ are sum of first $n$, $2n$ and $3n$ terms of an A.P. respectively then\n$$\nS_1 = \\frac{n}{2} [2a + (n - 1)d], \\quad S_2 = \\frac{2n}{2} [2a + (2n - 1)d], \\quad S_3 = \\frac{3n}{2} [2a + (3n - 1)d]\n$$\nConsider,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_2 - S_1 &amp;= \\frac{2n}{2} [2a + (2n - 1)d] - \\frac{n}{2} [2a + (n - 1)d] \n&amp;= \\frac{n}{2} \\left[ [4a + 2(2n - 1)d] - [2a + (n - 1)d] \\right]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_2 - S_1 &amp;= \\frac{n}{2} \\times [2a + (3n - 1)d] \n3(S_2 - S_1) &amp;= \\frac{3n}{2} [2a + (3n - 1)d] \n3(S_2 - S_1) &amp;= S_3\n\\end{aligned}$$"
                }
