            "type": "definition",
            "id": null,
            "title": "2.5.1 Congruence Modulo",
            "content": "Two integers a and b are congruence modulo n if they differ by an integer multiple of n; that is, a - b = kn for some integer k. This can also be written as a ≡ b (mod n).",
            "metadata": {},
            "sub_items": []
          },
          {
            "type": "example",
            "id": null,
            "title": "Example: congruence instance",
            "content": "For example, 61 ≡ 5 (mod 7) because 61 - 5 = 56 is divisible by 7.",
            "metadata": {
              "solution": "61 - 5 = 56 and 56 = 7 * 8, so 61 and 5 differ by a multiple of 7. Hence 61 ≡ 5 (mod 7)."
            },
            "sub_items": []
          },
          {
            "type": "note",
            "id": null,
            "title": "Note on remainders and modulo",
            "content": "- When a positive integer is divided by n, then the possible remainders are 0, 1, 2, ..., n - 1.\n- Thus, when we work with modulo n, we replace all the numbers by their remainders upon division by n, given by 0, 1, 2, 3, ..., n - 1.",
            "metadata": {},
            "sub_items": []
          },
